    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d451">

                <pb facs="0667" n="579" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg579"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wharton to Mr.
                            Hirsch.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-06-14"><hi rendition="#italic">June 14,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 342.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department was highly gratified to
                    learn by your No. 456, of 28th ultimo, that on the 15th of May, 1892, the Porte
                    issued a circular informing the local authorities that “until the framing of a
                    decision by the Government in explanation of the former instructions, the
                    present condition of schools and places of worship is not to be interfered
                    with.”</p>
                <p>Your action in the matter justly merits the fall approval of the Government.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William F. Wharton</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
